{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>You shall not go about as a talebearer.</b> That you should not say, if you are among the judges, 'I acquit but my colleagues are among those who convict, but what can I do, for my colleagues outnumber me.' Another interpretation of <b>לא תלך רכיל</b> — <b>לא תעמד</b>: had it said ולא תעמוד with a vav, I would say they are two commandments. Now that it is written לא without a vav, necessarily both are one commandment. And so the interpretation of <b>לא תלך רכיל</b> is so that you do not <b>stand against the blood of your fellow</b>. For when you say to your fellow, 'So-and-so spoke slander against you,' that person who was spoken about rises against him and kills him. And Ezekiel came and explained: 'men of talebearing were in you in order to shed blood.' Another interpretation: <b>לא תלך רכיל</b> — but if you heard of a plot against your fellow to kill him, do not stand idly by regarding his blood, rather inform him of the plot." "<b>לא תלך רכיל.</b> This refers to one who goes around with evil speech from this one to that one. Its Aramaic translation is לא תיכול קורצין — 'you shall not eat winks,' i.e., you shall not announce evil speech done by winking of the eyes. So too in Daniel: ואכלו קורציהון דיהודאי — 'and they ate the winks of the Jews,' meaning they announced. ירעם מן שמים — the Aramaic translation is אכלי מן שמיא, an expression of making a voice heard."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The phrase ויחזקאל בא ופי׳ introduces a prooftext from Ezekiel 22:9; Chizkuni routinely introduces such scriptural support without naming the book and chapter."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:13:33.314336454Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}